consiSt("ar",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Arkansas Supreme Court","d":"Hears appeals in the categories Supreme Court Rule 1-2(a) assigns to it (state constitutional questions, elections, writs against officials or circuit courts, attorney and judicial discipline, second appeals, and appeals required by law), takes cases reassigned from the Court of Appeals, and reviews Court of Appeals decisions only on discretionary petition for review. It also writes the court rules, controls bar admission and pro hac vice through its Clerk, and answers certified questions from federal courts."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Arkansas Court of Appeals","d":"The default appellate court: every civil appeal is filed there unless Rule 1-2(a) places it in the Supreme Court. Wrongly filed cases are transferred, not dismissed. There is no appeal of right from its decisions."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit courts","d":"The trial courts of general jurisdiction since Constitutional Amendment 80 merged law and equity. Each county has criminal, civil, juvenile, probate, and domestic relations divisions, but the divisions are for case management only and do not limit any circuit judge’s jurisdiction. Specialty dockets (drug, veterans, mental health courts) operate within divisions."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District courts (including small claims divisions)","d":"Under Administrative Order 18, district courts have exclusive jurisdiction of contract claims up to $100 and concurrent jurisdiction with circuit court of contract, personal-property recovery, and property-damage claims up to $5,000 (check the current statute, Ark. Code Ann. § 16-17-704, for any later change). The small claims division has the same limits, and lawyers may not take part. Procedure is governed by the separate District Court Rules (not collected)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Citizen Participation in Government Act","d":"Arkansas’s anti-SLAPP law is the Citizen Participation in Government Act, enacted by Act 1843 of 2005 as a new Subchapter 5 of Title 16, Chapter 63. It protects “privileged communications” and acts in furtherance of free speech or petitioning under the U.S. or Arkansas Constitution “in connection with an issue of public interest or concern.” Protected acts include statements made before or to a legislative, executive, or judicial proceeding, or about an issue under review by such a body. Privileged communications also include opinions and criticism of those proceedings and of public officers’ official acts. Statements made with knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard of falsity are excluded.\nThe text comes from the enacted session law on the Legislature’s site, not the current codified Code, which is hosted on a commercial site. Searches of later sessions found no amending bill, but confirm the current text before relying on it.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-63-501 to -508 (Act 1843 of 2005) §§ 501–503","v":true},{"t":"Immunity from civil liability","d":"A person making a privileged communication or a protected speech or petition act is immune from civil liability unless the statement was made knowing it was false or with reckless disregard of whether it was false.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-504 (Act 1843 of 2005)","v":true},{"t":"The mechanism is a verification requirement, not a special motion","d":"For any claim arising from a possible privileged communication or an act that could reasonably be seen as protected speech or petitioning, both the claimant and the claimant’s attorney must file, at the same time as the pleading, a written verification under oath. It must certify that they read the claim, that it is well grounded in fact and law after reasonable inquiry, that the act sued on is not a privileged communication, and that the claim is not brought for an improper purpose such as suppressing speech or petitioning, harassment, delay, or cost.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-505 (Act 1843 of 2005)","v":true},{"t":"Strike for no verification; 10-day cure","d":"An unverified claim must be stricken unless it is verified within 10 days after the omission is called to the attention of the claimant or the claimant’s attorney. If the claim is verified in violation of § 16-63-505, the court, on motion or on its own, must sanction the signers, the represented party, or both. Sanctions may include dismissal and reasonable expenses, including a reasonable attorney’s fee.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-506(a), (b)(1) (Act 1843 of 2005)","v":true},{"t":"Discovery stay and 30-day hearing","d":"Filing a motion to dismiss or strike under § 16-63-506 stays all discovery and pending hearings and motions. The hearing must be held within 30 days after service unless emergency matters require a later date. On motion and for good cause, the court may allow specified discovery or other proceedings to go forward. The Act sets no filing deadline for the motion.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. § 16-63-507 (Act 1843 of 2005)","v":true},{"t":"Fees and damages","d":"Fees come through the § 16-63-506(b)(1) sanction (reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees) for a falsely verified claim. Other compensatory damages require proof that the claim was brought or continued to harass, intimidate, punish, or maliciously inhibit protected speech or petitioning. The Act preserves any other recovery available by common law, statute, or rule.","c":"Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-63-506(b), -508 (Act 1843 of 2005)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: no listed interlocutory appeal","d":"The Act provides no immediate appeal, and Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a) does not list orders under the Act among the appealable interlocutory orders. Its immunity exception, 2(a)(10), covers only sovereign immunity and the immunity of government officials. Whether Arkansas appellate courts have allowed an interlocutory appeal of an anti-SLAPP ruling by case law was not checked.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a); Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-63-501 to -508","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: reciprocity, $200 per case, and an Arkansas sponsor","d":"Rule XIV applies only to a lawyer licensed in a state, D.C., or territory that would let an Arkansas lawyer appear in its courts. Pay $200 per case to the Clerk of the Arkansas Supreme Court first, then file in the trial court a sworn motion with proof of payment. The motion must include contact details, the associated Arkansas lawyer’s name and bar number, every Arkansas case in the past 2 years, all licensing jurisdictions and good standing, any discipline, any denial of admission or pro hac vice, and a statement of familiarity with the Arkansas Rules of Professional Conduct. The Arkansas lawyer must sign the motion and file an affidavit recommending admission. The fee is waived for indigent representation under Administrative Order 15.2.","c":"Ark. Bar Adm. R. XIV(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory cap: three Arkansas cases in 12 months","d":"The court must deny the motion if the lawyer has participated, served as counsel, or appeared pro hac vice in 3 Arkansas cases during the 12 months before the motion. The court may also examine the lawyer on Arkansas ethics and deny the motion for unauthorized practice or other good cause. Filing the motion submits the lawyer to the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court Committee on Professional Conduct, and misconduct can lead to revocation and contempt.","c":"Ark. Bar Adm. R. XIV(e)–(h)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission","d":"Arkansas has adopted the multijurisdictional-practice rule: a lawyer in good standing elsewhere may provide temporary services in association with an actively participating Arkansas lawyer, services related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, ADR-related services, and services arising out of the home-state practice. In-house and federally authorized practice is also allowed. No office or systematic and continuous presence is allowed.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 5.5(b)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Permanent admission routes","d":"Admission on motion requires an ABA-accredited J.D., active practice for 3 of the last 5 years, and reciprocity from the jurisdiction of the applicant’s principal practice for the prior 2 years. UBE score transfer requires a scaled score of at least 270 earned within the 36 months before applying.","c":"Ark. Bar Adm. R. XVI(1), XVIII(1)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are allowed by notice","d":"A lawyer may appear for a limited scope by filing and serving a notice of limited scope representation, and the lawyer’s role ends without leave on filing a notice of completion. Ghost-writing is allowed if the document carries the required notation naming the assisting Arkansas lawyer. Opposing counsel may contact the client directly unless written notice of the limited representation has been given, and then only outside the noticed matters.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 87; Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 4.2(b)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access and attorney-issued subpoenas","d":"E-filing uses the Administrative Office of the Courts’ system statewide. Each circuit or district court decides whether to adopt it, and once adopted it is exclusive, except for pro se filers and filers with special needs. Registered users consent to electronic service, and the e-filing fee is $20 to start a circuit or district court case or for appellate civil filings. Only an attorney “admitted to practice in this State” may issue a subpoena, so have Arkansas co-counsel sign subpoenas.","c":"Admin. Order No. 21 §§ 1(b), 3(a), 3(g), 7(a); Ark. R. Civ. P. 45(a)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code and currency","d":"The Arkansas Rules of Professional Conduct follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering, with Arkansas variations. The compilation collected (dated June 12, 2025) may not include amendments adopted December 11, 2025 (2025 Ark. 221), which the court’s amendments list shows took effect that day. Check that order. Discipline runs through the Supreme Court Committee on Professional Conduct and the Office of Professional Conduct.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct; 2025 Ark. 221 (not collected)","v":true},{"t":"Broader confidentiality exception for crimes","d":"Rule 1.6(b)(1) lets a lawyer reveal information to prevent “the commission of a criminal act” of any kind. The ABA rule is limited to reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The fraud exceptions in (b)(2)–(3) follow the ABA pattern.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Conflicts: related lawyers and no lateral screening","d":"Rule 1.8(l) bars a lawyer from opposing a party represented by the lawyer’s parent, child, sibling, or spouse without the client’s informed consent confirmed in writing. Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing costs and paying an indigent client’s costs but has no humanitarian-gift exception. Rule 1.10(a) has no provision letting a firm screen a lateral hire to avoid imputing the hire’s former-client conflict.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.8(e), 1.8(l), 1.10(a)","v":true},{"t":"Trust accounts and client files","d":"Trust records must be kept 5 years. No more than $500 of the lawyer’s own money may sit in trust to cover bank charges. Every trust account must report overdrafts to the Executive Director of the Office of Professional Conduct. Arkansas-only Rule 1.19 defines the client file, which the client gets on written request and which may not be held for unpaid copying costs. Files must be kept 5 years after the matter ends, and for life in criminal cases with death or life sentences.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.15(a)(3), (b)(3), (b)(5); 1.19","v":true},{"t":"Advertising and solicitation are stricter","d":"Ads must name at least one Arkansas-licensed lawyer and the office location. Ads may not use clients or former clients, and dramatizations are banned; actors must be identified. Targeted solicitation letters go by regular mail only, must say “ADVERTISEMENT” in red ink on the envelope and on each page, must carry the Committee on Professional Conduct complaint notice, and in death cases may not be sent until 30 days after the accident. Rule 7.4 limits claims of specialist certification.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 7.2(d)–(e), 7.3(b)–(c), 7.4(d)","v":true},{"t":"Fees and misconduct","d":"Contingent-fee agreements must be in writing and spell out the percentages at settlement, trial, and appeal, plus expenses and how they are deducted. Contingent fees are barred in divorce and support matters (except collecting amounts due under a final decree) and in criminal defense. Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination.","c":"Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c)–(d), 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Generative AI in the courts","d":"Administrative Order 25 (adopted December 11, 2025) warns that entering confidential or sealed client or court data into public generative-AI tools may violate Administrative Order 19, statutes, and the ethics rules. It bars court staff and clerks from exposing internal court data to public large language models. It is not a disclosure rule for filings.","c":"Admin. Order No. 25","v":true}],"style":"State of Arkansas","home":"https://www.arcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"120 days to serve, and extensions must be sought in time","d":"Unserved defendants are dismissed at 120 days. An extension motion must be filed within the 120 days (or the current extension), and the order must be entered within 30 days after the motion or by the end of the period, whichever is later. Default judgments based on certified mail need a return receipt signed by the addressee or agent; unclaimed mail is not refusal.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 4(g)(1)(A)(ii), 4(i)"},{"t":"Plead facts and attach the contract","d":"A complaint must state facts showing jurisdiction, venue, and entitlement to relief. Any written instrument a claim or defense is based on must be attached unless good cause is shown. An unspecified damages demand caps recovery below the federal diversity amount unless the demand shows more is sought.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 8(a), 10(d)"},{"t":"10-day motion responses, and hearings are waived","d":"Responses to motions are due 10 days after service and replies 5 days after the response. Each must include a brief supporting statement with citations or it can be struck. Unless counsel requests a hearing, the court can rule on the papers once the reply time runs.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 6(c), 7(b)(2), 78(c)"},{"t":"Jury demand by 20 days before trial","d":"There is no automatic jury. File a written demand no later than 20 days before the trial date or the jury is waived.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 38(a), (c)"},{"t":"Summary judgment cutoffs","d":"Summary-judgment motions must be filed at least 45 days before a scheduled trial absent leave. Responses are due 21 days after service, replies 14 days after the response, and no supplemental materials may follow the reply.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 56(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Document subpoenas: 3 business days’ notice to the other side first","d":"A subpoena for documents only must be served on all other parties at least 3 business days before it is served on the recipient, and everything produced must be shared promptly.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 45(b)(1)"},{"t":"Voluntary nonsuit: easy once, fatal twice","d":"A plaintiff may dismiss without prejudice as of right up to final submission, but only by court order. A second voluntary dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits unless all parties stipulate otherwise. Twelve months with no action of record invites dismissal for want of prosecution.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 41(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions die on day 30, and the appeal clock starts","d":"New-trial, JNOV, and amend-findings motions are due within 10 days after entry. If the judge has not ruled within 30 days after filing, the motion is deemed denied and the 30-day appeal period runs from that day, even if a hearing is scheduled later.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b)(2), 52(b)(1), 59(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)"},{"t":"Partial judgments need a Rule 54(b) certificate with findings","d":"Without an executed Rule 54(b) certificate stating specific factual findings, an order leaving any claim or party unresolved is not appealable. Request the certificate within 14 days of entry.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a)(11)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal must abandon unresolved claims","d":"A notice of appeal from a final order must state that the appellant abandons any pending but unresolved claim. That statement works as a dismissal with prejudice.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(e)(vi)"},{"t":"Directed-verdict motion at the close of all evidence","d":"In a jury case, a party without the burden of proof waives any sufficiency-of-the-evidence argument on appeal unless it moved for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence, not just the opponent’s case.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(e)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: three cases a year and reciprocity","d":"Arkansas courts must deny pro hac vice to a lawyer who appeared in 3 Arkansas cases in the prior 12 months. Lawyers from jurisdictions that do not admit Arkansas lawyers pro hac vice are not eligible. Each motion needs $200 paid in advance and a signed sponsor affidavit.","c":"Ark. Bar Adm. R. XIV(a)–(c), (f)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory change of judge; disqualification for cause only","d":"The collected rules give no right to a peremptory substitution of judge. A judge must disqualify where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including bias, family relationships to parties or lawyers, economic interest, prior involvement, or a public statement committing to a result. Paragraph (A)(4), the ABA ground for campaign contributions from a party or lawyer, is “[Reserved].” The comment says contributions do not by themselves disqualify. Except for bias, the parties may waive disqualification after disclosure, by agreement made without the judge present, and the agreement goes in the record. Statutory disqualification provisions were not collected.","c":"Ark. Code Jud. Conduct R. 2.11 & cmt. [4A]","v":true},{"t":"Replacement judges and appellate recusal","d":"When a judge is disqualified, the Chief Justice (or a designee) assigns a sitting or retired judge under Administrative Order 16, which replaced the former special-judge election process (Administrative Order 1 is repealed). In the appellate courts, a party moves for a justice’s or judge’s disqualification under Rule 2-1, stating the specific facts, a reasonable time before submission.","c":"Admin. Order No. 16 §§ I–II; Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 6-4","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Ark. Const. art. 2, § 6: whether it is broader than the First Amendment was not checked","d":"The Arkansas Constitution’s free speech and press guarantee was not among the collected texts, and no case law was reviewed. Do not assume protection beyond the First Amendment without research.","c":"Ark. Const. art. 2, § 6 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"No pauper status for libel, slander, or malicious prosecution plaintiffs","d":"Rule 72 bars bringing an action for slander, libel, or malicious prosecution in forma pauperis, so a speech-tort plaintiff must pay filing fees.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 72(d)","v":true},{"t":"Cameras need consent of the parties","d":"A judge may allow broadcasting or photographing, but a timely objection by any party or attorney prevents it, and a witness may refuse. Juvenile, probate, domestic relations, and drug court matters may never be broadcast.","c":"Admin. Order No. 6(b)–(c)","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Nine-of-twelve civil verdicts","d":"A civil verdict needs only 9 of 12 jurors, and every juror who agrees must sign.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 48","v":true},{"t":"Nonparty fault must be pleaded","d":"To have a jury or judge allocate fault to a nonparty in an injury, death, or property case, the defendant must identify the nonparty and plead facts in its answer (or an amendment) and then make a prima facie case. The plaintiff gets 30 days to add the nonparty.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 9(h), 49(c), 52(a)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Expert-consultation certification and no ex parte physician contact","d":"Signing a pleading certifies that, where a claim or defense requires expert proof, the party has consulted a qualified expert who finds a reasonable basis for it. Informal contact with an opposing party’s physician or psychotherapist is prohibited without that party’s express consent.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 11(b)(5), 35(c)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Legislator-lawyer continuances","d":"Cases with a legislator party or attorney are stayed from 15 days before a General Assembly session until at least 30 days after adjournment. Proceedings set on days when an attorney-member’s legislative committee meets are reset if the attorney asks at least 3 days ahead.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 40(c)","v":true},{"t":"Punitive damages trials are bifurcated on request","d":"On any party’s motion, if the evidence warrants, the jury decides compensatory liability and damages first and punitive damages in a second phase. Evidence of the defendant’s finances is kept out of phase one.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 42(b)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Supersedeas bonds capped at $25 million","d":"No appeal bond in a civil case may exceed $25,000,000 regardless of the judgment amount, unless the appellant is shown to be dissipating assets to avoid payment.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 8(c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Arkansas Rules of Appellate Procedure—Civil (current through October 1, 2026); Rules of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals of the State of Arkansas (current through December 15, 2025); Arkansas Rules of Civil Procedure (current through October 1, 2026), Rules 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62; Administrative Orders No. 2 and No. 21 (current through January 29, 2026). All from the Arkansas Judiciary’s official Court Rules and Administrative Orders pages (opinions.arcourts.gov). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Arkansas: 30 days from the clerk’s file-stamp, and a short fuse on post-trial motions","d":"Civil appeals go to the Arkansas Court of Appeals unless Supreme Court Rule 1-2(a) sends them to the Supreme Court, which can also reassign cases and reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review. An appeal is taken by filing a notice of appeal with the circuit clerk within 30 days after the judgment is entered, and a judgment is entered when the clerk file-stamps it.\nPost-trial motions are due within 10 days after entry, and they are deemed denied on the 30th day after filing if the judge does not rule. The appeal clock then restarts automatically, whether or not anyone noticed. The record must be lodged within 90 days of the first notice of appeal, and briefing runs from the lodging date. Briefs are word-limited and filed electronically.\nIf the order does not resolve every claim against every party, there is no appeal unless a listed interlocutory exception applies or the circuit court signs a Rule 54(b) certificate with specific factual findings.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2, 3, 4, 5; Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 1-2, 4-2, 4-4; Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b); Admin. Order No. 2(b)"},"groups":[["Which court, and what is appealable",[{"t":"Default court is the Court of Appeals","d":"Every appeal is filed in the Court of Appeals except cases on the Supreme Court’s list: interpretation of the Arkansas Constitution, death or life sentences, writs against state, county, or municipal officials or circuit courts, elections, attorney and judicial discipline, second or later appeals after a Supreme Court decision, and appeals required by law. The Supreme Court may reassign cases involving first impressions, conflicts, federal constitutional questions, substantial public interest, or the validity or construction of statutes and rules. A case filed in the wrong court is transferred, not dismissed.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 1-2(a)–(c), (e)"},{"t":"Final judgments and the listed interlocutory orders","d":"Appeal lies from a final judgment, an order that ends the action, and listed orders: grant or refusal of a new trial; an order striking an answer or pleading; orders on attachment or garnishment; injunction and receivership orders; an order disqualifying an attorney; an order granting or denying class certification; denial of a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment based on sovereign immunity or a government official’s immunity; arbitration orders under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-108-228; all probate orders except two narrow types; and a contempt order that imposes a sanction and finally disposes of the contempt. Final custody orders are appealable.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a), (d)"},{"t":"Partial judgments need a Rule 54(b) certificate with findings","d":"An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is not final unless the circuit court executes the Rule 54(b) certificate, stating specific factual findings that there is no just reason for delay. A party wanting a certificate must move within 14 days after entry; the motion is deemed denied if no certificate is entered within 30 days, and no certificate may be entered more than 45 days after the judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later. The appeal time runs from entry of the certificate. Claims against named but unserved defendants, including “John Doe” defendants, are dismissed by the final judgment.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b)(1)–(6); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a)(11), 4(a)(1)(B)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal must abandon pending unresolved claims","d":"The notice must state that the appellant abandons any pending but unresolved claim, and that statement operates as a dismissal with prejudice as of the date the appealed order was entered. The statement is not required for interlocutory appeals under Rule 2(a)(2)–(13), juvenile appeals, or Rule 54(b) partial judgments.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(e)(vi)"},{"t":"Discretionary interlocutory appeal of privilege discovery orders","d":"The Supreme Court may allow an appeal from an order denying a protective order, compelling production, or refusing to quash a subpoena when the objection is a privilege or opinion work product. The petition is due within 14 calendar days after entry, is limited to 10 pages plus an addendum, and must address six factors; the circuit court’s order must contain findings on the same factors. A response is due within 10 calendar days; no reply or rehearing. If granted, file the notice of appeal within 10 calendar days and the record within 30 calendar days of the order. Proceedings are not stayed unless a court orders.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(f); Ark. R. Civ. P. 26(f)(2)"},{"t":"Extraordinary writs","d":"Prohibition, mandamus, and certiorari petitions are filed in the Supreme Court with the certified record, and are not available where appeal is an adequate remedy. Name the other parties, not the judge, as respondents, and give the judge a copy. Responses are due in 10 calendar days; petitions and responses are limited to 15 pages excluding the addendum.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 6-1(a), (e)"}]],["Notice of appeal and cross-appeal",[{"t":"30 days from entry, and entry means the clerk’s file-stamp","d":"File the notice of appeal with the circuit clerk (or the county clerk, where that clerk serves as ex officio circuit clerk) within 30 days after entry of the judgment or the Rule 54(b) certificate. A judgment is entered when the clerk stamps it with the date, time, and “filed,” regardless of when it is recorded. A judge may make an order effective at once by signing it and marking it “filed in open court” or “filed by Judge,” but it must be file-marked by the next business day, and that date controls the appeal deadlines. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed the day after entry.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(b), 4(a), 4(d); Admin. Order No. 2(b)(2), (b)(4); Ark. R. Civ. P. 58"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"The notice must name the appealing parties, designate the order appealed, designate the record contents, state that the transcript has been ordered and paid for under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-13-510(c), state whether the appeal goes to the Court of Appeals or the Supreme Court (citing the Rule 1-2(a) ground), and include the abandonment statement. If less than the full record is designated, serve a statement of the points to be relied on.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(e), 3(g)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"File a notice of cross-appeal with the circuit clerk within the later of 10 days after the notice of appeal is filed or 30 days after entry of the order. An appellee who wants more of the record designates it within 10 days after the notice of appeal is filed.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 3(d), 4(a)(2), 6(b)"},{"t":"Failure to receive notice of the judgment","d":"If a party shows it did not receive notice of the judgment, counsel was diligent, and no one is prejudiced, the circuit court must extend the appeal time by 14 days from entry of the extension order, but only on a motion filed within 180 days after entry of the judgment.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3)"}]],["Post-trial motions and the deemed-denied trap",[{"t":"10 days to file; 30 days to rule; then the clock runs","d":"A timely JNOV motion, motion to amend findings, new-trial motion, or any other motion to vacate, alter, or amend filed within 10 days after entry extends the appeal time for all parties. The notice of appeal is due 30 days after the order disposing of the last motion. But if the court does not rule within 30 days after the motion is filed, it is deemed denied on the 30th day and the 30-day appeal period starts then, even if the judge later holds a hearing or rules.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1); Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b)(2), 52(b)(1), 59(b)"},{"t":"Notices filed while a motion is pending","d":"A notice of appeal filed before the motion is decided is treated as filed the day after the disposing order or the deemed-denial date, and it covers the underlying judgment. To also challenge the ruling on the motion, amend the notice within 30 days; no extra fee.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(2)"},{"t":"Rule 60 relief is separate","d":"Within 90 days after the judgment is filed, the circuit court may vacate or modify it to correct errors or prevent a miscarriage of justice; after 90 days, only the grounds listed in Rule 60(c) apply. A Rule 60 motion does not affect finality or suspend the judgment. A misprision of the clerk (including a judgment entered before the answer was due) is not a ground for appeal until relief is sought in the circuit court.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 60(a), (c), (h), (k)"},{"t":"New-trial motion not required to preserve error","d":"A party that has preserved an error need not move for a new trial to raise it on appeal. But in a jury case, a party without the burden of proof must move for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence to challenge sufficiency on appeal.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 59(f), 50(e)"}]],["Record on appeal",[{"t":"Lodge within 90 days of the first notice of appeal","d":"The appellant files the certified record with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 90 days after the first notice of appeal; for injunction or receivership appeals under Rule 2(a)(6)–(7), within 30 days. The circuit court may extend the time, before it expires, on the appellant’s motion and specific findings (including that the transcript was timely ordered and paid for), but never beyond 7 months from the first notice of appeal. After that, or if an extension order cannot be obtained, petition the Supreme Court for certiorari before the deadline. A late-tendered record requires a motion for rule on clerk.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 5(a)–(b); Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-2, 3-5"},{"t":"Electronic record format","d":"The record is a searchable, bookmarked PDF whose page numbers match the record pages, with the transcript as a separate file and files under 30 megabytes each. The appellant transmits it electronically after certification. Cite the record as (RP page) for the clerk’s portion and (RT page) for the transcript.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 7(b)–(c) (electronic-record version); Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-2(f)"},{"t":"Supersedeas and the $25 million cap","d":"A stay requires a supersedeas, issued by the circuit clerk (or the appellate clerk once the record is lodged) on an approved bond. No bond in a civil action may exceed $25,000,000 regardless of the judgment, unless the court finds by a preponderance that the appellant is dissipating assets to evade payment. Execution is automatically stayed only for 10 days after entry; injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed during an appeal unless the court orders otherwise. The State needs no bond.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 8(a)–(c); Ark. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d), (e)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Schedule runs from lodging the record","d":"Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is lodged. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant’s brief. Reply: 15 days after the appellee’s brief; a cross-appellant’s reply 15 days after the cross-appellee’s brief. The Clerk may grant one 7-calendar-day extension on oral or electronic request, which must be confirmed by letter; further extensions need a written motion showing good cause, and stipulations are not recognized. If the appellant’s brief is not filed, the appeal may be dismissed.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(b)–(d), (h), 4-5"},{"t":"Word limits and required contents","d":"Appellant and appellee briefs: 8,600 words; reply: 2,875; appellee/cross-appellant: 14,325; reply/cross-appellee: 11,475. Only the jurisdictional statement, statement of the case and facts, argument, and request for relief count. Each brief needs a cover, table of contents, points on appeal, table of authorities, jurisdictional statement (finality, timeliness dates, and which court under Rule 1-2), statement of the case and facts with record cites, argument with the standard of review for each point, request for relief, certificate of service, and a certificate of compliance with Administrative Order 19, Administrative Order 21 § 9 (no hyperlinks to outside material), and the word count.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-2(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Format","d":"File a single word-searchable PDF converted from the word processor (not scanned), double-spaced, at least 14-point proportionally spaced serif type, one-inch margins (two inches at the top of the cover), paginated with the cover as page one and bookmarked for each section. Noncompliant briefs are rejected; a substantially compliant brief is marked “tendered” with 7 days to fix.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-1, 4-4(g)(1)"},{"t":"Oral argument by letter request","d":"Request oral argument by a separate letter filed with the party’s initial or reply brief. It is allowed on request unless the appeal is frivolous, the issue has been authoritatively decided, or argument would not help.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-1(a)"},{"t":"Citing opinions","d":"Opinions issued after July 1, 2009 are precedent and may be cited; earlier opinions not designated for publication may not be cited except in related litigation. Opinions since February 14, 2009 use the public-domain form, for example 2010 Ark. 171 or 2010 Ark. App. 745, with pinpoint cites and the parallel regional-reporter or database cite.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-2(b)–(d)"}]],["Motions, rehearing, and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Motions","d":"Motions, petitions, and responses are filed electronically (except case-initiating filings, pro se filers, and those with special needs) and are limited to 10 double-spaced pages. Responses are due within 10 calendar days. A motion to reconsider an order on a motion is due within 18 calendar days.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(a), (d), (g), (h)"},{"t":"Petition for rehearing: 18 calendar days, one shot","d":"File within 18 calendar days after the decision, with a supporting brief, proof of service, and a certificate that it is not for delay; 10 pages maximum. The response is due the following Monday (Supreme Court) or Wednesday (Court of Appeals) or within 7 calendar days, whichever is later. Only one petition per party; no oral argument; new counsel may not be substituted for the purpose. A fact claimed to be overlooked must have been clearly referenced in the statement of the case and facts.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-3"},{"t":"Petition for review of a Court of Appeals decision","d":"There is no appeal of right from the Court of Appeals. A petition for review is due within 10 calendar days after the Court of Appeals’ rehearing period ends, is limited to 3 pages, and must allege a tie vote, a conflict with a published opinion, or error tied to a Rule 1-2(b) ground; briefs are not accepted. A response is due within 10 calendar days. If review is granted, a party may request oral argument and supplemental briefing within 10 days (supplemental briefs are limited to 10 pages).","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 1-2(d), 2-4"},{"t":"Mandate","d":"The mandate issues when the decision is final, meaning after the rehearing or review period, and not within 18 calendar days absent leave or stipulation. A stay of mandate pending a U.S. Supreme Court certiorari petition lasts up to 90 days unless extended.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 5-3"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"The Arkansas Supreme Court may answer questions of Arkansas law certified by a federal court; it decides whether to accept within 30 days of the certification order, and inaction for 30 days is a declination.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 6-8(a)"}]],["E-filing",[{"t":"Appellate filings are electronic","d":"Briefs, motions, and petitions are filed through the Administrative Office of the Courts’ e-filing system with no paper copies; pro se filers and persons with disabilities may file on paper. The e-filing fee in the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals is $20.00 for civil actions. An appellate e-filing is considered filed when transmission is complete, subject to the clerk’s review and acceptance, and anything received before midnight is filed that day.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(a), 4-4(a); Admin. Order No. 21 §§ 3(g)(2)(A), 6(a)(2), 6(d)"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals","d":"The appellate courts must sanction frivolous appeals, improper filings, and delay, with sanctions including dismissal, fees, penalties, and damages. A sanctions response is due within 21 days.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 11"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal","d":"30 days from entry (clerk’s file-stamp) of the judgment or order, or entry of the Rule 54(b) certificate.\nFile with the circuit clerk. Must include the abandonment statement for final orders.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(1), 4(d); Admin. Order No. 2(b)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry)","note":"File with the circuit clerk. Must include the abandonment statement for final orders."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or 30 days from entry of the order, whichever is later).\nUse the later of the two dates.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Use the later of the two dates."}},{"t":"Post-trial motions (JNOV, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nDeemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days after filing.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days after filing."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a post-trial motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order deciding the last motion, or the deemed-denial date (30th day after filing).\nThe deemed-denial date runs automatically.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order deciding the last motion, or the deemed-denial date)","note":"The deemed-denial date runs automatically."}},{"t":"Amend notice to appeal the post-trial ruling","d":"30 days from disposition or deemed denial of the motion.\nNo additional fee.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (disposition or deemed denial of the motion)","note":"No additional fee."}},{"t":"Motion to extend appeal time for non-receipt of notice of judgment","d":"180 days from entry of the judgment.\nRelief is 14 days from entry of the extension order.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3)","due":{"n":180,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment)","note":"Relief is 14 days from entry of the extension order."}},{"t":"Rule 54(b) certificate motion","d":"14 days from entry of the partial judgment.\nDeemed denied at 30 days; no certificate later than 45 days after judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later.","c":"Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the partial judgment)","note":"Deemed denied at 30 days; no certificate later than 45 days after judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal privilege discovery order","d":"14 days from entry of the order.\nCalendar days; 10-page limit; response in 10 calendar days.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(f)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Calendar days; 10-page limit; response in 10 calendar days."}},{"t":"Appellee’s designation of additional record","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 6(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Lodge the record","d":"90 days from filing of the first notice of appeal.\n30 days for injunction and receivership appeals; circuit court extensions capped at 7 months from the first notice.","c":"Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 5(a)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"30 days for injunction and receivership appeals; circuit court extensions capped at 7 months from the first notice."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"40 days from lodging of the record.\nOne 7-day clerk extension available.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(b)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (lodging of the record)","note":"One 7-day clerk extension available."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from filing of the appellant’s brief.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"15 days from filing of the appellee’s brief.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(d)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellee’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Response to a motion or petition","d":"10 days from filing of the motion or petition.\nCalendar days.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(d)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion or petition)","note":"Calendar days."}},{"t":"Motion to reconsider an order on a motion","d":"18 days from date of the order.\nCalendar days.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(g)","due":{"n":18,"from":"the trigger date (date of the order)","note":"Calendar days."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"18 days from date of the decision.\nCalendar days; 10-page limit; one petition only.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-3(a)","due":{"n":18,"from":"the trigger date (date of the decision)","note":"Calendar days; 10-page limit; one petition only."}},{"t":"Petition for review in the Supreme Court","d":"10 days from end of the Court of Appeals rehearing period.\nCalendar days; 3-page limit. An early petition is held as tendered.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (end of the Court of Appeals rehearing period)","note":"Calendar days; 3-page limit. An early petition is held as tendered."}},{"t":"Response to petition for review","d":"10 days from filing (or deemed filing) of the petition.\nCalendar days.","c":"Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(e)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing)","note":"Calendar days."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal",30,"Entry (clerk’s file-stamp) of the judgment or order, or entry of the Rule 54(b) certificate","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(1), 4(d); Admin. Order No. 2(b)(2)","File with the circuit clerk. Must include the abandonment statement for final orders."],["Notice of cross-appeal",10,"Filing of the notice of appeal (or 30 days from entry of the order, whichever is later)","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(a)(2)","Use the later of the two dates."],["Post-trial motions (JNOV, amend findings, new trial, alter or amend)",10,"Entry of judgment","Ark. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b); Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","Deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days after filing."],["Notice of appeal after a post-trial motion",30,"Entry of the order deciding the last motion, or the deemed-denial date (30th day after filing)","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(1)","The deemed-denial date runs automatically."],["Amend notice to appeal the post-trial ruling",30,"Disposition or deemed denial of the motion","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(2)","No additional fee."],["Motion to extend appeal time for non-receipt of notice of judgment",180,"Entry of the judgment","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 4(b)(3)","Relief is 14 days from entry of the extension order."],["Rule 54(b) certificate motion",14,"Entry of the partial judgment","Ark. R. Civ. P. 54(b)(2)","Deemed denied at 30 days; no certificate later than 45 days after judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later."],["Petition for permission to appeal privilege discovery order",14,"Entry of the order","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(f)(1)","Calendar days; 10-page limit; response in 10 calendar days."],["Appellee’s designation of additional record",10,"Filing of the notice of appeal","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 6(b)",""],["Lodge the record",90,"Filing of the first notice of appeal","Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 5(a)","30 days for injunction and receivership appeals; circuit court extensions capped at 7 months from the first notice."],["Appellant’s brief",40,"Lodging of the record","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(b)","One 7-day clerk extension available."],["Appellee’s brief",30,"Filing of the appellant’s brief","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(c)",""],["Reply brief",15,"Filing of the appellee’s brief","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 4-4(d)",""],["Response to a motion or petition",10,"Filing of the motion or petition","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(d)","Calendar days."],["Motion to reconsider an order on a motion",18,"Date of the order","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-1(g)","Calendar days."],["Petition for rehearing",18,"Date of the decision","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-3(a)","Calendar days; 10-page limit; one petition only."],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court",10,"End of the Court of Appeals rehearing period","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(a)","Calendar days; 3-page limit. An early petition is held as tendered."],["Response to petition for review",10,"Filing (or deemed filing) of the petition","Ark. Sup. Ct. R. 2-4(e)","Calendar days."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.arcourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Arkansas Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"Govern all civil suits and actions in the circuit courts, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.” Law and equity are merged in the circuit courts under Constitutional Amendment 80.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement; “clerk” defined; separate actions and fees; medical-injury notice","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint with the clerk, who notes the date and precise time. A refiled case gets a new number and a new filing fee; no other claim (such as divorce or paternity) may be joined to a Domestic Abuse Act petition, and an adoption cannot be sought inside a guardianship case. Subdivision (d) requires 60 days’ written notice to each medical-care provider before a medical-injury suit, but it applies only after the General Assembly enacts a statute giving a 90-day limitations extension for notices served within the last 60 days; whether that statute exists was not checked.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons and service of process","s":"The clerk issues the summons on filing; process is the summons plus complaint. Personal service rules cover individuals, minors, incarcerated persons, corporations, LLCs, partnerships, governments, and others; alternatives are certified mail restricted to the addressee (a default needs a return receipt signed by the addressee or agent, and unclaimed mail is not refusal), first-class mail with notice and acknowledgment (fails if no acknowledgment within 20 days), approved commercial delivery, warning order (published weekly for 2 consecutive weeks; defendant has 30 days), and court-ordered service after diligent effort. Service must be made within 120 days of filing or the action is dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant; an extension motion must be filed within the 120 days (or a prior extension) and the order entered within 30 days after the motion or by the end of the period, whichever is later. Substantial compliance is enough if the defendant had actual notice and timely answered, and any waiver of service defects must be in writing and filed.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint are served on every party (on the attorney if represented) by delivery, regular mail, approved commercial delivery, or e-mail to an attorney; mail service is complete on mailing. Discovery is not filed unless the court orders. Confidential information under Administrative Order 19 must be left out or redacted, with an unredacted copy filed under seal. Clerks with fax machines must accept faxed filings ($1.00 per page). Every paper needs a certificate of service stating date and method.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day; if the last day is a weekend, legal holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is closed, run to the next open day; for periods under 14 days, skip intermediate weekends and holidays. Motions and notice of hearing must be served 20 days before the hearing, responses are due 10 days after service of the motion, and replies 5 days after the response. Add 3 business days after service by mail, commercial delivery, or electronic means, but not to the answer period after mail service of the summons. The court cannot extend deadlines under Rules 4(i), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), and 60(b) except as those rules allow.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings and motions","s":"Only a complaint, answer, counterclaim reply, cross-claim answer, third-party complaint, and third-party answer are allowed. Every written motion, response, and reply must include a brief supporting statement of facts, law, and citations, with affidavits served at the same time, or it may be struck; the court need not grant an unopposed motion. Dispositive-motion submission follows Administrative Order 3(2)(B), and one extra copy of each pleading and motion is filed for the court.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim must state, in ordinary and concise language, “facts” showing jurisdiction, venue, and entitlement to relief. A demand for unliquidated damages that states no amount limits recovery to less than the federal diversity amount unless the demand shows more is sought. The affirmative-defense list includes comparative fault and the workers’ compensation exclusive remedy, and averments other than damages are admitted if not denied.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity is denied by specific negative averment; fraud, mistake, duress, and undue influence must be pleaded with particularity. Subdivision (h) requires a defendant who wants a jury to allocate fault to a nonparty under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-61-202(c) to identify the nonparty and plead facts showing fault in the first responsive pleading (or an amendment once known); the plaintiff then has 30 days to amend to sue the nonparty.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption with court, title, case number, and designation; the complaint names all parties. Pleadings must show the signing attorney’s name, bar number, mailing address, telephone, fax, and e-mail. Averments go in numbered paragraphs, and a copy of any written instrument on which a claim or defense is based must be attached unless good cause is shown.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; sanctions","s":"Every paper is signed by an attorney of record (except limited-scope drafting under Rule 87) and certifies proper purpose, legal support, and factual support. The certificate also covers (b)(5), requiring a party whose claim or defense needs expert proof to have consulted an expert who finds a reasonable basis, and (b)(6), redaction of confidential information. Sanctions are mandatory when a violation is found; a sanctions motion must be served 21 days before filing (safe harbor), and a show-cause order must allow at least 14 days.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer within 30 days after service; 30 days from first publication or posting after a warning order; 60 days for a defendant incarcerated in Arkansas; 30 days for counterclaims and cross-claims; 30 days after notice of a federal remand order is filed. After a motion is denied or a more definite statement is served, plead within 10 days. Rule 12(b)(6) tests “failure to state facts,” and Arkansas adds 12(b)(8) (the Reporter’s Notes call it the one addition to the federal list), pendency of another action between the same parties on the same transaction, which is waived if not raised with the other personal defenses. A motion to strike may be filed within 30 days after service if no response is permitted.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, with the usual exceptions for claims in another pending action or where the opposing party used attachment. Permissive counterclaims and cross-claims against co-parties follow the federal pattern; an omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment under Rule 15.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may implead a person who is or may be liable to it without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 10 days after the answer; otherwise leave is needed on notice to all parties. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead on the same terms.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend at any time without leave, except to add Rule 12(h)(1) defenses, but the court may strike the amendment or grant a continuance on motion for prejudice or undue delay. Respond within the remaining time or 20 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Relation back for a changed party requires notice and knowledge within the Rule 4(i) service period (120 days); supplemental pleadings also need no leave.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial procedure","s":"The court may hold a pretrial conference on simplifying issues, amendments, admissions, limiting experts, referral to a master, and settlement or mediation under Ark. Code Ann. § 16-7-202, and enters an order that controls unless modified to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution. A guardian sues or defends for a minor or incompetent person; otherwise a next friend or guardian ad litem acts, and no judgment may be rendered against a minor or incompetent person until a guardian or guardian ad litem has defended.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opponent, subject to Rule 13(a). Any claim may be severed, and the court may transfer a claim to another division in the interest of justice; a money claim and a fraudulent-conveyance claim may be joined before judgment on the money claim.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Persons needed for complete relief or with an interest that could be impaired must be joined if subject to service; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs prejudice, shaping of relief, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy to decide whether to proceed or dismiss. Subject to Rule 23.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; the court may order separate trials to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by order at any stage on just terms, and claims may be severed.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants exposing the plaintiff to multiple liability may be required to interplead, even if claims lack common origin. A disinterested stakeholder who deposits the money or property in the registry is discharged and may be awarded reasonable litigation expenses, including attorney’s fees.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus a finding that common questions predominate and a class action is superior; Arkansas uses this single predominance-and-superiority test for every class. Notice in money-relief classes must be the best practicable; costs of notice fall on the representatives but may be shifted on settlement or substantial success. Settlements, dismissals, and compromises of certified classes need court approval after notice and a hearing. An order granting or denying certification is immediately appealable under Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(a)(9).","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Actions by shareholders","s":"Derivative complaints must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership, and plead with particularity the demand made on directors (and members, if necessary) or why none was made; dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Members may sue or be sued as a class through named representatives only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members; Rule 23(d) and (e) procedures apply.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right on an unconditional statutory right or an impaired, inadequately represented interest; permissive intervention on a conditional statutory right or common question, including by agencies administering a relied-upon statute. The motion must attach the pleading, and the court may require notice to the Attorney General when a state statute’s constitutionality is challenged.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After death, substitution must be moved within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record by service of a statement, or the action may be dismissed as to the decedent. The court may appoint a special administrator only for the litigation if there is no general personal representative. Guardians, transferees, and successor public officers may be substituted; the rule does not revive claims barred by limitations or non-claim.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. The rule contains no initial-disclosure or discovery-conference requirement and no expert-report requirement; expert discovery is by interrogatory and deposition of testifying experts. Inadvertent production is presumed not to waive privilege if the producing party gives notice within 14 calendar days of discovering it, and the receiving party must return, sequester, or destroy within 14 calendar days of notice. Protective-order motions require a good-faith conference statement. Orders compelling production over a privilege or opinion-work-product objection must contain findings on six listed factors, and the Supreme Court may allow an interlocutory appeal under Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 2(f).","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"26.1","t":"Electronic discovery","s":"An optional rule that applies only if the parties agree or the court orders it on motion for good cause, made within 120 days after the complaint is filed. The parties confer within 30 days and report a plan within 14 days after the conference. It covers preservation, form of production, inaccessible sources (burden on the objecting party), cost allocation, and a safe harbor for information lost through routine good-faith operation of a system.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition in the county of an expected adverse party’s residence; notice must be served at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of a summons. Depositions may also be taken pending appeal on motion in the circuit court.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee; abroad on notice, by commission, by treaty, or by letter of request. Arkansas adds rules for foreign proceedings seeking Arkansas evidence and bars preferred-provider arrangements and any restriction on an attorney’s choice of court reporter.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may stipulate in writing to deposition arrangements and to modify other discovery procedures.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"The plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after service on any defendant unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given that the witness is leaving the state or country. The text sets no limit on the number or length of depositions (the Reporter’s Notes say the federal limit on the number of depositions was not adopted), but the court may limit time by order. Objections must be concise, non-argumentative, and non-suggestive, and instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, a court limit, or a motion to terminate. If review is requested before the deposition ends, the deponent has 30 days after notice to review and sign changes. Remote depositions are allowed by stipulation or order.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"No leave needed except for prisoners or early plaintiff depositions. Cross questions within 14 days after notice, redirect within 7 days after cross, and recross within 7 days after redirect.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party present or noticed: by any party to impeach; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent, or designee; and for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, or cannot be subpoenaed. A deposition is not usable against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order. A video deposition offered in a jury trial must be shown in nonstenographic form on request.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Answers or objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant gets the later of 30 days after service or 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. The text sets no numeric limit on interrogatories. Blanket objections are not recognized, grounds must be stated with particularity, and unstated grounds are waived; each interrogatory must be repeated before its answer.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and entry on land","s":"Responses are due in 30 days (a defendant gets the later of 30 days or 45 days after service of process). Objections must be specific and particular, blanket objections are not recognized, and an objection must say whether responsive materials are being withheld. Documents are produced as kept or organized by request category.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause, by a physician or psychologist. Under subdivision (c), a party relying on a physical, mental, or emotional condition must sign a medical-records authorization within 30 days after request, and informal ex parte contact with another party’s physician or psychotherapist is prohibited without that party’s express consent.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days (a defendant gets the later of 30 days or 45 days after service of process). Requests must be in a separate, titled document not combined with other discovery; each request must be repeated before the answer; blanket objections are not recognized. An attorney’s signature on answers counts as the attorney’s oath and authority to bind the client.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel require a good-faith conference statement; expenses are awarded to the winner unless the position was substantially justified. Failure to obey an order allows issue-establishing, preclusion, striking, dismissal, default, and contempt sanctions. Arkansas adds (e), sanctions for prejudicial failure to supplement under Rule 26(e), and (f), no expenses or fees against the State except as a statute allows.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"A jury must be demanded in writing at any time after commencement and no later than 20 days before the trial date; others may demand on remaining issues within 10 days. Failure to demand waives the jury, and a demand cannot be withdrawn without the parties’ consent.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Issues demanded are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no jury right exists; otherwise the court tries them but may order a jury trial on motion. Advisory juries and consent juries are allowed.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"40","t":"Trial settings and continuances","s":"Cases are set on request after issues are joined, or by the court. Continuances require a motion and good cause. Proceedings are stayed from 15 days before through at least 30 days after a General Assembly session when a party, an attorney, or certain officers are legislators, and are reset on legislative-committee meeting days if the attorney-member asks at least 3 days ahead.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may take a voluntary nonsuit as of right any time before final submission to the jury or court, effective only on entry of an order (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 41 rejects the federal limits and follows prior Arkansas law). A second voluntary dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits unless all parties stipulate otherwise. After 12 months with no action of record, the court must give notice and dismiss for want of prosecution unless good cause is shown; that dismissal is without prejudice unless the action was previously dismissed. Costs of a prior dismissed action may be ordered and the new case stayed until paid.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may consolidate actions sharing common questions and may order separate trials. In jury cases seeking punitive damages, on any party’s motion and if the evidence warrants, the trial must be bifurcated before the same jury, and evidence of the defendant’s financial condition is excluded from the first phase unless relevant to another issue.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony","s":"Testimony is taken in open court unless the rules (including Rule 88 on virtual and blended proceedings) or law provide otherwise; affirmation may replace an oath; motions may be heard on affidavits; the court may appoint and pay interpreters, with costs taxable.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Domestic records by official publication or attested copy with a custodian certificate; foreign records by attested copy with final certification (or treaty certification); a certificate of no record proves absence; other methods allowed by law remain available.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party raising the law of another jurisdiction (including another state) must give notice in a pleading or other reasonable written notice. The court decides it, may consider any relevant source, and the ruling is reviewed as a question of law.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk, or an attorney “admitted to practice in this State” who is counsel of record, may issue subpoenas; the text does not extend attorney-issued subpoenas to lawyers appearing pro hac vice, so have Arkansas co-counsel sign them. A documents-only subpoena must be served on all other parties by e-mail, fax, or hand delivery at least 3 business days before it is served on the recipient, and all produced material must be shared promptly. A trial or hearing subpoena must be served at least 2 days before attendance and a deposition subpoena at least 5 business days before, each with a fee of $30.00 per day and $0.25 per mile. A recipient may object in writing to document production within 10 days after service (or by the compliance date if sooner). A deposition witness can be required to attend within 100 miles of residence, employment, or business; trial witnesses must attend anywhere in the state.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"45.1","t":"Subpoena for interstate depositions and discovery","s":"A party in an out-of-state case submits the foreign subpoena to the circuit clerk in the county where discovery is sought; the clerk issues an Arkansas subpoena without opening a case or charging more than the statutory fee, and this is not an appearance. Objections are due within 10 days after service; enforcement, quash, and protective-order motions are filed in that county, which then opens a case and charges the fee.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making the desired ruling or objection and its grounds known at the time preserves the issue.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court either lets counsel examine the venire or examines it itself and allows supplemental questioning. Up to 2 alternates may be seated, with 1 extra peremptory challenge per side usable only on alternates.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict","s":"A civil verdict needs agreement of 9 of 12 jurors (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 48 follows prior Arkansas law, not FRCP 48). The parties may stipulate to fewer jurors and a stated majority. All consenting jurors sign a non-unanimous verdict; the foreperson signs a unanimous one.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"49","t":"Verdicts and interrogatories","s":"General verdicts, general verdicts with interrogatories, and special verdicts follow the federal pattern. Subdivision (c) adds allocation of fault in personal-injury, medical-injury, wrongful-death, and property-damage cases among all persons, including nonparties, but a nonparty’s fault may be assessed only if the nonparty settled or Rule 9(h) notice was given, and the defendant made a prima facie case.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"50","t":"Directed verdict and JNOV","s":"Arkansas keeps the “directed verdict” and “judgment notwithstanding the verdict” labels. A JNOV motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment and it is deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days of filing. A party without the burden of proof must move for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence to preserve a sufficiency challenge in a jury case; a party opposing a JNOV that was granted may move for new trial within 10 days.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions; objections","s":"Requested instructions are submitted at the close of evidence or earlier as directed; the court instructs before closing argument. To preserve error, object before or when the instruction is given, stating the matter and grounds, and to complain of a failure to instruct, a party must have proffered an instruction on that issue.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In nonjury cases the court makes special findings only if a party requests them before entry of judgment. Findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous (clearly against the preponderance of the evidence). Bench trials also allocate fault among parties and nonparties under (a)(2), on the same conditions as Rule 49(c). A motion to amend findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment and is deemed denied after 30 days.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Reference is the exception and only where no jury right exists or it has been waived; outside accounts and damage computations, an exceptional condition must be shown. The court accepts a master’s findings unless clearly erroneous; objections are due within 20 days after notice of filing the report.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"A partial judgment is final only with an executed Rule 54(b) certificate stating specific factual findings that there is no just reason for delay; without it, the order is not appealable. A motion for a certificate must be filed within 14 days after entry; it is deemed denied if not entered within 30 days of the motion, and no certificate may be entered more than 45 days after the judgment or 30 days after the motion, whichever is later. A final judgment dismisses claims against named but unserved (including “John Doe”) defendants. Costs are awarded only if the court directs (no clerk taxation) and are limited to a closed list; attorney’s fee motions are due 14 days after entry of judgment.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"Default judgment is entered by the court; the rule provides no clerk’s default (the Reporter’s Notes say Rule 55 varies substantially from FRCP 55). A party that has appeared gets written notice at least 3 days before the hearing. A default may be set aside for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, voidness, fraud, or any other reason justifying relief, but except for a void judgment the movant must show a meritorious defense. No default may be entered against a party that answered or filed a Rule 12 motion in federal court before remand.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement; any summary-judgment motion must be filed at least 45 days before a scheduled trial absent leave for good cause. The response is due 21 days after service of the motion and the reply 14 days after the response; no supplemental materials after the reply without leave; any hearing must be at least 7 days after the reply deadline. The standard is “no genuine issue as to any material fact,” and the opponent must set forth specific facts by affidavit or otherwise.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Actions under Ark. Code Ann. §§ 16-111-101 to -111 follow these rules, with jury rights under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may advance the case.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Every judgment or decree must be on a separate document and is effective only when entered as provided in Administrative Order No. 2, which makes entry the clerk’s file-stamp of date, time, and “filed.” The court may have the prevailing party prepare the form and may enter it without opposing counsel’s consent; costs taxation does not delay entry.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials","s":"Eight listed grounds, including verdict clearly contrary to the preponderance of the evidence and newly discovered evidence. The motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment, is deemed denied if not ruled on within 30 days of filing, and grounds (a)(2), (3), and (7) need supporting affidavits; opposing affidavits are due 10 days after service, extendable up to 20 more days. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days. A new-trial motion is not required to preserve error for appeal.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment, decree, or order","s":"Within 90 days after filing with the clerk, the court may modify or vacate a judgment for any reason to correct errors or prevent a miscarriage of justice. Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time. After 90 days, only listed grounds apply: newly discovered grounds (within 1 year after discovery or judgment, whichever is earlier), constructively summoned defendants (within 2 years after the judgment or 1 year after service of a certified copy, whichever is earlier), clerk misprisions, fraud by an adverse party, errors against infants or persons of unsound mind, death of a party, and infancy (within 12 months after turning 18). The movant must show a valid defense or cause of action. The Reporter’s Notes describe the rule as substantially different from FRCP 60.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution for 10 days after entry unless ordered; injunction and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay pending post-trial motions; a supersedeas bond, given with or after the notice of appeal, stays the judgment (bond terms and the $25 million cap are in Ark. R. App. P.–Civ. 8). The State and its agencies need no bond.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability of a judge","s":"If the trial judge cannot perform post-verdict or post-findings duties, another judge sitting in or assigned to that court may perform them or grant a new trial if unable to do so.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"64","t":"Addition and withdrawal of counsel","s":"New counsel must promptly enter an appearance with the clerk and notify the court and opposing counsel. Withdrawal requires court permission for good cause on a motion showing steps to avoid prejudice, readiness to return papers and property, and refund of any unearned fee, except limited-scope withdrawal under Rule 87.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and temporary restraining orders","s":"Preliminary injunctions only on notice; a TRO without notice needs specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney certification of notice efforts, expires after no more than 14 days unless extended once for good cause or by consent, and can be challenged on 2 days’ notice. Security is required for any TRO or preliminary injunction, but the State, its officers, and agencies are exempt. Every order must state reasons, specific terms, and the acts restrained without reference to other documents. Statutory emergency relief follows subdivision (b) unless the statute provides otherwise.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security; proceedings against sureties","s":"Sureties submit to the court’s jurisdiction and appoint the clerk as agent; liability may be enforced by motion.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"Circuit courts may appoint receivers when necessary; the receiver gives bond and takes an oath, reports every 6 months or as directed, may hire professionals with approval, and may be removed for good cause. An action with a receiver cannot be dismissed without a court order.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"By leave and on notice, a party may deposit money or deliverable things; withdrawal only on express written order to the person the court finds entitled.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; acceptance must be served within 10 days. If the offeree’s final judgment, exclusive of interest from the date of the offer, is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer, and “costs” means reasonable litigation expenses excluding attorney’s fees (broader than taxable costs).","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution discovery","s":"A judgment creditor or successor of record may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, under these rules. The rule does not itself set execution procedure.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it at that party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or enter a judgment divesting and vesting title to property within its jurisdiction.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for or against nonparties","s":"A nonparty may enforce an order in its favor, and be compelled to obey an enforceable order, by the same process as a party.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"72","t":"Suits in forma pauperis","s":"An indigent person petitions with a verified affidavit in the prescribed form and a copy of the complaint; the court finds indigency (it may consult federal poverty guidelines) and, if there is a colorable cause of action, waives clerk’s fees. No action for slander, libel, or malicious prosecution may be brought in forma pauperis.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"73–76","t":"(not in compilation)","s":"Rules 73 through 76 do not appear in the October 1, 2026 compilation collected from the judiciary’s website; no rule text exists to summarize.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"77","t":"Courts and clerks","s":"Courts are deemed open for filings and process during clerk’s office business hours; trials and hearings are public except as provided by law (for example, Ark. Code Ann. § 16-13-222). The clerk’s office is open business hours except weekends and legal holidays, and the clerk may grant routine process motions subject to the court’s review.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"78","t":"Motion day and hearings on motions","s":"Courts set regular motion times where practicable. A hearing may be held only after the reply time expires; unless counsel requests or the court orders a hearing, it is waived and the court may rule on the papers. Election-related mandamus or prohibition petitions must be set for hearing no sooner than 2 and no later than 7 days after filing.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"79","t":"[Abolished]","s":"Marked “[Abolished]” in the compilation.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"80","t":"Admissibility of testimony at prior trial","s":"When admissible, testimony from a former trial between the same parties or privies on the same issue may be proved by a certified transcript.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of rules","s":"The rules apply to all civil proceedings in circuit court except where a statute creating a right, remedy, or proceeding specifically provides a different procedure; they also apply to civil appeals tried de novo in a court of record. Where no procedure is prescribed, the court proceeds in any lawful manner.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit circuit-court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"83","t":"[Abolished]","s":"Marked “[Abolished]” in the compilation. The Reporter’s Notes to Rule 30 state that local rules are not permissible in Arkansas.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"84","t":"Uniform paper size","s":"All papers are on 8½ × 11 inch paper.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"Cited as the Arkansas Rules of Civil Procedure (ARCP).","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"The rules took effect July 1, 1979.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"87","t":"Limited scope representation","s":"Consistent with Ark. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.2(c), a lawyer may limit an appearance by filing and serving a notice of limited scope representation. A lawyer who only drafts papers for a self-represented person need not sign them but must add the notation that the document was prepared with the assistance of a named licensed Arkansas lawyer under Rule 1.2(c), and may rely on the client’s facts unless the lawyer has reason to believe them false. The role ends, without leave, on filing a notice of completion; service on the lawyer is required only within the noticed scope.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"},{"n":"88","t":"Virtual and blended court proceedings","s":"Hearings and nonjury trials may be fully virtual or blended if the court finds rights are protected, a record can be kept, evidence procedures and technology are adequate, and the public has access (a virtual hearing needs a courtroom notice posted at least 1 hour before). Jury trials may be blended only for specific witnesses or the court reporter; parties, counsel, judge, and jury must be in the courtroom. Requests come by joint motion, a party’s motion showing the safeguards, or the court’s notice; recordings by anyone but the court are barred except as Administrative Order No. 6 allows. Adopted effective June 1, 2022.","u":"https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/cr/en/item/16712/index.do"}]});
